Mercy did not run down the hill. Running was for children and the guilty. She walked, back straight, and every step jarred her teeth.

Behind her, she could feel the hill. The sun on one side. The shadow on the other. She had stood in the shadow. She had chosen it. She would choose it again.

Her finger found the hole in her apron. She didn't remember making it. She worked a thread loose and wound it tight around her fingertip until the skin went purple. Then she let it go.

The vicarage kitchen was cold. No one had banked the fire. She knelt at the hearth and did it herself, the way she did everything herself. Ash, kindling, flint. The spark caught on the third strike. She did not look at the flame.

She filled the basin. She took the lye soap from the shelf. She scrubbed her hands. Then her wrists. Then she pushed her sleeves back to the elbows and scrubbed her forearms, where the scars were. Ten thin white lines on each arm. Four long ones, and one short where a little thumb had dug in. She knew them better than the lines on her own palms.

The lye bit. She scrubbed harder. The water turned pink.

She had not touched anything on that hill. She scrubbed anyway.

--

The door banged open. Mud came in first, then a dog, then a boy.

"Tobin. Boots."

He didn't hear her. He never heard her anymore. Notty shook himself and sprayed the clean floor with gray water. Tobin climbed onto the bench with his feet still on.

"Mam. Mam, did you see? Everyone saw. Arne says the bone man made them go to sleep. Proper sleep. In the ground. Arne's da was there and he said one of them turned into a person again, just for a bit, and then it was dust, and it sparkled." He pulled a crust of bread from his pocket, gray with lint, and bit it. "Arne says it was beautiful."

Mercy pulled her sleeves down. She dried her hands on her apron. Her thumb found the hole.

"Arne's da," she said, "drinks."

"Arne says --"

"Arne isn't your mam."

Tobin chewed. He looked at her the way the Vicar looked at the sky before rain. Notty lay down under the bench and put his snout on his paws and watched her too.

"Was Da there?"

The fire popped. She jumped. She hated that she jumped.

"Your da is in the ground," she said. "Where he belongs."

"But was he --"

"Boots, Tobin."

He slid off the bench. He pulled his boots off one at a time and set them by the door, neat, side by side, the way she had taught him. He had not done that in a year. She stared at them. Mud seeped from the soles into the floorboards.

"I'm sorry, Mam," he said.

She opened her mouth. Nothing came out. She closed it.

He took Notty and went upstairs. The dog's claws clicked on every step.

She looked at the boots for a long time.

--

A year ago, the dead had come down the hill at night.

She had heard the screaming before she understood it. She had grabbed Tobin from his bed and run, because everyone was running. She had tangled in her nightdress. She had fallen in the square. And when she rolled over, Wystan was standing above her.

He had dirt in his beard. He had dirt in his eyes. His eyes were open, and there was nothing in them. Nothing at all. He had been a gentle man. He had been a wizard who made the lamps in the mine glow orange so the men could see. He had sung, badly. He had never once raised a hand to her.

He reached for her throat.

She had wrapped herself around Tobin, and Tobin had screamed and clawed at her arms to get free, and she had held on, and held on, and held on. She had screamed at her husband. She did not remember what she screamed.

Then men with torches. Men with pickaxes. Matthis, young and gray-faced, a rope in his hands. Somebody dragged Wystan off her.

They had put him in the ground again. In a box. In chains. He clawed out in a week.

Then they had put him in a cage.

And today she had watched him walk up the hill in the sun, white and clean, and lie down on his own grave like a man lying down in his own bed. She had watched the Bone Man kneel beside him. She had watched him turn to dust and glitter.

She had not crossed into the light. Maud had. Maud had held Kendrick's hand. Maud had smiled.

Mercy had stood in the shadow and called the Bone Man a monster, and she had meant it, and she still meant it.

She went back to the basin. The water was cold now. She scrubbed again.

--

The Vicar sat in his study with the curtains drawn. A single candle. His hands lay folded on the desk, but they shook. They had shaken since this morning. He did not look up when she came in.

"Supper, Vicar."

"Not hungry, Mercy. Thank you."

She set the bowl down anyway. Barley and onion. He would eat it later when he thought she wasn't looking. He always did.

"Did you see," he said to the candle, "their faces?"

"I saw a man playing with our dead."

"Maud's face." He rubbed his eyes. "I have stood over that woman's husband three times now. Once at his funeral. Once when he came out of the ground. Once at the gallows, every Sabbath, to pray for him. Not once did she look like that." He unfolded his hands. Folded them again. "Not once did I give her that."

"You gave her the truth," said Mercy. "That he was gone."

"He was not gone. That was the trouble, wasn't it?"

Mercy did not answer. She straightened a stack of letters on the corner of the desk. Most were in her hand. The Vicar's letters to the diocese. The Vicar's letters to the Miner's Guild. The paper Matthis had signed yesterday. Corven had praised the Vicar's neat handwriting. The Vicar had not corrected him. Neither had she.

A knock at the front door.

They both stiffened. Nobody knocked at the vicarage after dark. People came in, or they stayed away.

--

The little balding man stood on the step with his clay tablet clutched to his chest. He was already talking when she opened the door.

"Goodwife, I do apologize for the hour, though I would point out that the hour is not of my choosing, as the Guild master insisted on showing me his ledgers before supper, every last one of them, in an order that I can only describe as geological, which is to say whatever landed on top last, and I would further point out that it is very difficult to take a proper accounting of a town where nobody keeps one, and so here I am, at a vicarage, after dark, in the mud." He drew a breath. "Pellin. Of the Collegium. We require testimony."

Behind him, a man in white stood at the bottom of the step.

He did not hold the horse's reins. The horse stood on its own in the middle of the square, head up, ears forward, still as a carving. The man's hands were folded loosely in front of him. The lamplight from the doorway reached his robe and stopped there, as though it did not dare go further.

He was looking at the gallows. In the dark, the empty gibbets were only black shapes against black. One of them still dripped. Mercy could hear it from here. Ploink. Ploink.

Then he turned and looked at her.

"May we come in, Mercy?"

She had not told him her name. Her hand tightened on the door.

He waited. He did not step forward. He did not lean. He simply waited, as though he had all the night in the world and would gladly give her every hour of it. She found she had stepped aside.

--

She put them in the kitchen, because the Vicar would not leave his study, and because the kitchen was hers.

Notty came halfway down the stairs, hackles up, a growl already in his throat. The man in white glanced at him. Just a glance. The dog's ears went flat. He whined, low and thin, and lay down on the step with his belly to the wood. Above him, a small pale face peered through the rails.

"Tobin," Mercy said. "Bed."

Tobin did not move. He never moved when she said it.

The man in white looked up at him and nodded. Only that. A small nod, the kind a father might give across a crowded room.

Tobin went. Notty slunk after him. A door closed upstairs.

Mercy stared at the empty stairs.

The man in white crossed to the hearth. He drew off his gloves, one finger at a time, and held his bare hands to the fire. They were clean hands. Not a callous on them. Not a fleck of coal.

"You keep a good fire," he said.

She did not know why that should make her throat ache.

Pellin had already claimed the table. He laid out his tablet, a stylus, a small pot of water, a smaller pot of something gray, a square of linen, and a second stylus, which he lined up beside the first and then adjusted a hair's width to the left.

"Clay," he said, without being asked, "because vellum smudges, and paper is a frivolity of the very rich and the very foolish, and I have learned in thirty-one years of service, Goodwife, that the very rich and the very foolish are frequently the same people, and that neither of them can be trusted with a permanent record. Clay can be smoothed and rewritten until it is correct, and then it can be fired, and then it is correct forever. There is no greater virtue in a material." He wet the stylus. "From the beginning, then. The disaster. Twenty-three dead, I am told."

"Twenty-two."

Pellin's stylus stopped. He looked up at her. His little mole's eyes went round.

"The Guild master's ledger says twenty-three."

"The Guild master counted Old Dunstan twice. Once as a hauler and once as a mule driver. He was both. He only died once."

Pellin's mouth opened. Something like joy crossed his face. "He counted him twice."

"On two different pages."

"Two different pages." Pellin pressed a hand flat on the table, as though to steady it. "Do you know, Goodwife, that in the town of Harrowgate, where I was posted for nine years, nine, the Reeve recorded a flood in his tithe book and his grain book and his book of deaths, all three, with three different dates, and when I asked him which was correct he told me the flood had lasted a week and so they were all correct, which is not how floods work, or how dates work, or how books work --"

"The Vicar's predecessor," said Mercy, "recorded every burial by the day of the week. Not the date. Only the day."

"Only the --" Pellin put down his stylus. "But how would anyone ever --"

"They couldn't. I had to go back through the stonecutter's receipts to --"

"The stonecutter's receipts. Goodwife, you are wasted in this kitchen. Wasted. Twenty-two." He scratched furiously at the clay, smoothed, scratched again. "Twenty-two dead, seventeen interred and remaining interred, five interred and not remaining interred, which is a phrase I have now written in four separate towns, and every time I write it I am told it was the mine, or the weather, or the will of heaven, and nobody, nobody, files a declaration --"

"Pellin."

The man in white had not turned from the fire. He had not raised his voice. He had only said the name.

Pellin stopped mid-word. His mouth stayed open. Then it closed.

Mercy realized she had been leaning across the table. Her hands were flat on the wood, close to the tablet, close to Pellin's. She drew them back into her lap. Her face was warm.

The man in white turned around. The fire behind him made his robe glow orange, like a mine lamp, and left his face in shadow. Only his eyes caught the light. Gray. Like ash.

"The five," he said. "Their names."

"Osric. Alrik. Kendrick. Hollis." She swallowed. "Wystan."

He did not ask her relation. He did not need to. He looked at her the way no one had looked at her in a year. Not the way the Vicar looked at her, as though she were a pair of hands. Not the way the village looked at her, as though she were a shrill noise. He looked at her as though she were the only person in Coalridge who had been right all along.

"You were there," he said. "That night. In the square."

It was not a question. She nodded.

"And today, on the hill, you did not go to him."

Her thumb found the hole in her apron. She nodded again.

"Good," he said.

The word went through her like cold water. She did not know if it had comforted her or cut her. Both. She wanted him to keep looking at her. She wanted him to stop.

Pellin cleared his throat, very softly, as though asking permission to exist. "The Bone Man. Matthis. Did he cause the rising?"

Mercy opened her mouth.

She thought of Matthis at the gallows with the rope that night. Young and gray-faced. She thought of him rolling the gibbet across the square, alone, breath heaving, while miners walked past and pretended not to see. She thought of Maud's face.

"He touched them," she said. "Every day. With his hands. He kept them. He fed them. He talked to them. Nobody else did that. And now look what he can do."

Pellin's stylus scratched. "That is not, strictly speaking, an answer, Goodwife, though I grant it is more useful than most of the answers I have received today, including the smith's, which was a lecture on the proper weight of a hammer --"

"It is an answer," said the man in white.

Pellin fell silent.

"It is the truest one anyone has given us." The man in white sat down across from her. He folded his clean hands on her table, next to the bread knife. "Tell me what you want, Mercy."

Nobody had asked her that. Not in a year. Not the Vicar. Not Corven. Not Tobin. Not Wystan, before.

"I want them to stay in the ground."

"They are in the ground," he said gently. "As of this afternoon. All but three."

The fire popped. She did not jump this time.

"Then I want to know why," she said. "Why they came up. Why they went down. Why it was him. Why it wasn't the Vicar, or me. I want it written down. All of it. So it never surprises anyone again."

The man in white smiled. It was a small smile, warm, and it did not reach his eyes, and she found she did not care.

"Then you shall have it," he said.

He looked at Pellin. Pellin was already rummaging in his satchel.

"The Collegium will require a registrar in this valley, which is to say a person of good character and legible hand who will keep a full and faithful account of every casting, every effect, every practitioner, every unregistered practitioner, which in my experience is every practitioner, and every irregularity of any kind, cross-referenced by date and not, I would stress, by day of the week --" Pellin glanced at her, and she almost smiled "-- and submitted quarterly in duplicate, one copy fired and one unfired, to allow for corrections, though there should be no corrections, though there always are --"

The man in white lifted one finger from the table. Pellin stopped and unrolled the vellum.

"Vellum," said Mercy. "I thought it smudged."

"It does," said Pellin, with deep bitterness. "But it is what the Collegium has always used for appointments, and the Collegium has always used it because it has always used it, and I have filed eleven petitions on the subject --"

"Every name, Mercy," said the man in white. "Every one. Nothing forgotten. Nothing left in a cage for a year because no one knew what else to do."

She looked at her hands. Red and raw from the lye. Steady.

She dipped the quill. She signed her name in her own hand. Neat. Every letter perfect.

Pellin leaned over the vellum and made a small, satisfied sound.

The man in white rose. He drew on his gloves, one finger at a time. At the door, he stopped by Tobin's boots. One had tipped over in the mud. He bent and set it upright beside the other.

"Goodnight, Mercy."

And then he was gone, and Pellin was gone, still talking, and the horse turned its head in the square to follow them without anyone touching it.

--

She did not go to bed.

She took a lamp. She left it unlit. She walked through the dark square, past the church, past the smithy. Past the gallows, where the last of the blood and rust dripped from a cage with nothing in it. Ploink. Ploink. She did not look at it.

The cemetery hill was silver in the moonlight. No shadow line now. No sun side. It was all one color.

She found Wystan's grave without the lamp. She had always been able to find it. The wooden marker had been straightened. Moss brushed off. The Bone Man's work. She knew his hands now.

The grass on the mound shone. Faint. Like frost, but it wasn't cold. She knelt. Her knees sank into the wet earth.

She held her hand above the grass. It shook. She held it there until it stopped.

She touched the grass.

Dust clung to her fingertips. It did not glitter in the moonlight. It was just dust. Gray. Fine. It smelled like nothing at all.

She waited for it to move. To twitch. To crawl toward her like beetles with half their legs gone.

It didn't.

She waited a long time.

Then she wiped her fingers on her apron, over the hole, and stood, and walked down the hill. At the bottom, she stopped. She looked at her fingers. A little gray still clung to the nail of her thumb.

She did not scrub it off.
